I think most people are just questioning a six-inch tread.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.000
That's enormous!

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.000
What kind of tyre is that?

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.000
It's not six inches.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.000
That's two or three inches at most.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.000
No, I don't know anything about cars.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
LAUGHTER

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
This is going to make no sense now, but anyway...

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
But today we're looking beyond the tyre.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Or indeed...

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.000
LAUGHTER

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.000
..we're going through the tyre,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
which was one of Lloyd Grosman's less successful animal-based shows.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Because we're going to look at what we can learn from

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
and I will use more Radio 4 language than my colleague here,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.000
the romantic behaviour of monkeys.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
We will be asking about the monogamy of the mandrill,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
the gregariousness of the gibbon and the temptation of the tamarin.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Do monkeys appear to fall in love?

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.000
How does sibling bonding vary?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.000
What gets a monkey hot under the furry collar?

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
And how often do we see them exhibit same-sex behaviour?

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Ultimately, what do we learn about ourselves

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
by learning about the love life of monkeys?

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
To aid and abet us, we are joined by a psychologist,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
an anthropologist and a cruciverbalist.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.000
And they are...

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Hello, I'm Robin Dunbar.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
I'm a professor of evolutionary psychology at the University of Oxford

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
and I've spent most of my life studying monkeys and humans and feral goats.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.000
LAUGHTER

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.000
And I think the most surprising thing that I've seen, really, with monkeys

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.000
is just how Machiavellian and scheming they can actually be.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.000
They really are like watching humans.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Hello, I'm Jo Setchell.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.000
I'm a professor of anthropology at Durham University.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.000
I study mandrills, who are a very large, very colourful species of monkey

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.000
that live in the rainforest of Gabon in Central Africa.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
And the most peculiar thing that I have learned from studying those monkeys

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
is that they use their vibrant colour to avoid conflict

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
and that they have a scent gland on their chest

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.000
which they rub against trees to advertise who they are,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.000
how high-ranking they are and even their DNA.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
My name's Dave Gorman.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.000
I am what I am and what I am needs no excuses.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.000
LAUGHTER

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.000
I first learned that a mandrill is a type of monkey and not a euphemism.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
LAUGHTER

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.000
And the thing I found most edifying from watching monkeys

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.000
is the knowledge that they are literally too busy singing to put anybody down.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.000
LAUGHTER

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.000
And this is our panel.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
APPLAUSE

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Let's first of all just start off, Jo,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.000
in terms of what is the kind of variety of, I suppose,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.000
well, romantic relationships that we see in monkeys, or sexual?

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
It's hugely varied.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
So if we think about primates, all of the primates,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
it can be from a long-term bond that lasts decades

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.000
through to a relationship that lasts seconds.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.000
The cutest relationship, I think, is the titi monkey.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.000
So they're one of the ones that form very, very long-term pairs.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
And as far as we know, which is not very far,

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.000
but as far as we know they're relatively monogamous in those pairs.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.000
And they sit in trees and twine their tails around one another.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.000
That's a romantic relationship.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.000
They sit together and twine their tails around each other.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
They're a very cryptic species which means they hide a lot.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.000
So they hide in tangles of vines in the trees.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.000
But they always forget their tails.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
So you walk around in the forest

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.000
and just see these two tails hanging out of the forest.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
And you know where the titi monkeys are.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.000
I think you're imposing some sort of cultural paradigm

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.000
where you say they twine their tails and that's romantic.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Because you might also say that's jealous and possessive.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.000
That's I always know where you are.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.000
And also if you always forget your tail,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.000
to combine your tail with someone else's tail

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.000
will improve the likelihood of remembering your tail.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Absolutely.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.000
Robin, what about for you in terms of looking at what we might see?

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Let's say romantic.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.000
Because even defining that can be quite difficult, can't it?

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.000
We project something of ourselves onto that behaviour.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.000
It's actually much easier to define friendships in monkeys.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Because their friendships are very similar to our kind of friendships.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.000
The way they set them up.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.000
I guess romantic relationships, they kind of look the same.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
But I mean it does vary enormously from species to species.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.000
And even within a species.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.000
Well just like humans I suppose.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Individuals have different intensities of relationships.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.000
They're introverts and extroverts.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.000
From an evolutionary perspective, Joe.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.000
You talked about this whole variety of relationships.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Polygamy, monogamy.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.000
Is there anything that's favoured evolutionarily?

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Can we say it would be better if a species only had monogamous relationships?

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Long-term relationships?

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.000
Or what would you say?

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.000
A large number of two-second relationships?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.000
They're all favoured evolutionarily, which is why they exist.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.000
But the kind of boring ecological answer is that it depends on the distribution of food.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
And it depends how many females can live in a group.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.000
And how many females can live in a group determines how many males can be added to that group.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.000
So if there's enough food for just one animal, then a female has to live on her own.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Or she'll have her kid with her.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.000
But no one else.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.000
If anyone else tries to join her, make a group, then there wouldn't be enough food.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.000
It does get more complicated than this, but that's the basic.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:31.000
Then if you can have two females living together, then they might allow a male to join them.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.000
There are advantages to having a male join you.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.000
You avoid harassment from other males.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.000
You probably do better in terms of protection from predators eating you.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.000
But at the same time, the bigger the group, the more likely the predator is to find you.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.000
And then if there's plenty of food, then you can have a large group of animals.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:55.000
And when there's a large group of females, no one male can control access to them.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.000
It's complete chaos.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.000
It is complete chaos, exactly.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.000
With a bit of order underneath.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.000
Is monogamy more prevalent in a smaller group or in a larger group?

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.000
In a small group.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:12.000
So where you have just one female, she might choose to share her area, we call it home range, with one male.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.000
And she might also choose to only reproduce with that one male.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:21.000
But she could also share her home range with one male and reproduce with other males.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.000
Okay.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.000
So Robin, you were just mentioning there the chaos.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Do you find yourself sometimes, say you're out on a Friday night, and you look out the street and you think,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:34.000
how much am I learning about the monkeys by watching the humans,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.000
in the same way as I learn about humans from watching the monkeys?

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:43.000
Actually, to be fair, a lot of the stuff that we've spent the last 20 years doing

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.000
has been on humans in order to understand monkeys better.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.000
Because you can do things with humans that you can't do with monkeys.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.000
You can stick humans in neuroimaging machines and stuff like that.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.000
And you can ask them questions.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Give me an example of one experiment where you might think,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
right, if we do that to some humans, that will help us understand this particular group of monkeys more.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.000
Okay.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.000
So the number of friends you can have is limited basically by the size of your brain.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:15.000
That's a generic relationship across mammals as a whole, basically.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.000
But in primates, it takes a very quantitative form.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:26.000
What we were able to do with humans originally was to look at your personal social network

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.000
and tie that to the size of different bits of your brain.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.000
So it's a very, very strong relationship.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.000
So we don't actually need to ask you how many friends you've got, Robin.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.000
We just need to look at your brain and we can tell you.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.000
This is one of your most famous and most quoted pieces of research.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.000
So tell us, yes, that it is known as Dunbar's number.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.000
Dunbar's number, yes.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.000
That's the limit on the number of meaningful relationships you can have, friends and family,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.000
and that's about 150 in humans.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:03.000
That actually consists of a series of layers of friendship, of greater and greater intimacy,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.000
as they come in towards you.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.000
You've got a small group of very close intimate friendships

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.000
and then bigger and bigger circles of less intimate friendships.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.000
So the average is 150.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:20.000
But how you are between 100 and 250 correlates with the size of particularly this part of your brain

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.000
but also bits around here.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:25.000
That's the frontal cortex.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.000
Yes, it's prefrontal cortex but also the temporal lobe.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.000
And then inside that there's this massive wiring connection

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.000
and it's the default mode neural network.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.000
And they called it the default mode neural network because they had no idea what it did.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.000
But when you put people in the scanner, brain scanner,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.000
and told them to relax and not do anything,

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.000
then this network became very, very active.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.000
And being as though a neuroscientist,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.000
apologies to neuroscientists in the audience,

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:58.000
and didn't actually know what was going on inside people's heads,

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.000
they thought it must be the brain daydreaming.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:06.000
And it's turned out, basically, when you're put in that sort of situation, what do you do?

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.000
You think about friends, family, relationships, the social network.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.000
So the thing is going crazy because it's thinking about the network.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.000
And then since we did those original,

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.000
there have been about 25 studies now showing this in humans.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.000
Neuroimaging experiments have been done on three groups of monkeys.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.000
So the same effect is being shown at the individual level.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.000
So through neuroimaging of like Dave or Brian or Joe or me,

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:35.000
would we be able to see how shallow our friendships were as well?

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.000
Yes, exactly. Or even if you have any.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.000
So how does that scale?

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.000
So if you go to, let's say, a macaque or a pika species,

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.000
then how does it change?

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.000
Does it change in a linear fashion if it's twice the size?

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.000
So you'll like this little bit because it's mathematical.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:58.000
And by the way, if you want any evidence of how actually functional that is,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
that it really exists, this number,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.000
somebody did an analysis of 61 million Facebook pages,

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.000
counting all the friends on each of these 61 million Facebook pages.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.000
The average was 149.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.000
They've made the great mistake there of assuming

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.000
that the people I'm Facebook friends with are people I'm friends with.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.000
In general, they are.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:26.000
Your 150 friends, as I mentioned earlier, are divided up into a series of layers.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.000
Those layers have very, very specific numbers.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:32.000
They're 5, 15, 50, and 150.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.000
So the 5 is what we call your shoulders to cry on friends.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.000
They're the ones that will, when your world falls apart,

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.000
they will drop everything and come and pick you up again.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:47.000
Turns out that those numbers are optima for the efficiency

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.000
with which information flows through networks.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:57.000
And what's more, those are the numbers you find not only between species of primates

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:59.000
but also within primate groups.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:03.000
The structure of primate groups has exactly those numbers as well.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.000
So they don't run to the 150 because they don't have as big a brain as us.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:11.000
But for those species like baboons and macaques and chimpanzees

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:15.000
that live in groups of 40 or 50 individuals,

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:20.000
they're substructured in exactly the same way as human social networks are.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.000
What are the other forms of behaviour that we might expect to be different

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:29.000
depending on the cognitive ability to have that many social connections?

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:32.000
You've got what I call the line dancing problem.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:38.000
Imagine you're in a group, you're foraging through the grasslands or the woodlands

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:42.000
or the savannah or the forest or whatever it is.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:50.000
How many people can you have in a group and still have people at either end in time on a line dance?

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.000
And the answer is, without music, very small.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:59.000
And that's the problem, the synchrony problem of moving in the same direction.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:04.000
It's really hard work for monkeys and apes and the few other species of mammals

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:08.000
that have stable groups to try and keep the group together.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:13.000
And initially they do it just by keeping track of their neighbour.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:21.000
But that will only really allow you to work with up to a group of about 15, maybe, maximum.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.000
And that will be about five females and maybe three males,

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:28.000
something of that sort of size, and then the rest are kids.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.000
And you have to break through what's a glass ceiling at that point and produce something else.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:36.000
The producing something else has to do with grooming bonds.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.000
So at that point they start to invest very heavily in social grooming

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:47.000
and create these really intense friendships, bonded relationships that keep individuals together.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:51.000
So your friends will keep checking on you as well as you keep checking on them.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.000
And that will do you up to about 30, groups of 30.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:59.000
And after 30 there seems to be another glass ceiling and you have to go through that.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:02.000
And that's when they start to use serious cognition.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:10.000
The brain really starts to produce major new kinds of cognitive strategies

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:14.000
which allow them to figure out who's doing what with who, basically.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.000
I think I've just realised why S Club 7 split up.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:24.000
Because each of them realised that only five of the others would pick them up when they were down.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.000
And resented the other two.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Although I am assuming they were all capable of serious cognition, so maybe that's not right.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.000
How much does that affect, say, the Osmonds?

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:37.000
Because there also you've got an actual genetic link as well,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.000
which would probably change the relationship, wouldn't it?

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.000
Well, how much does it affect all family bonds if only five people are allowed in that inner circle?

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:48.000
The minute you've got a six-person family, someone's in trouble.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:54.000
The way we've talked about it so far is quite mathematical, it seems to me.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.000
But when we talk about, of course, human relationships, there's a large amount of choice involved.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:03.000
So I suppose I'm asking questions like, is there a pin-up monkey?

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.000
Is there a handsome monkey that everyone would go for?

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:10.000
Why are you bringing up, is there a pin-up handsome monkey, Brian?

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:14.000
I just wonder if there's any self-interest here that we're watching.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:17.000
It's because it's written down here on the script.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:21.000
You wrote that, I remember you doing that, crossing out the other one.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.000
Is there a monkey that wears a card?

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.000
And you went, oh, I don't want to do that.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:33.000
So when I started studying mandrills, I was attracted to them because they're very colourful.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.000
But I wanted to know whether there's a pin-up effect.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:43.000
So whether the most colourful animal is the most impressive, perhaps attractive to females and so on.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:46.000
And I've been studying them now for decades.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:55.000
And initially, I did discover that the pin-up animal, the most colourful animal, has the highest testosterone, is the highest ranking.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
And I thought, oh yeah, so I'm really figuring out something here.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.000
He's probably the most important.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:03.000
And females do like to hang around the most colourful animal.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.000
But there are many other things that come into it too.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
So there is his colour.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.000
There's also how nice he is.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:15.000
So if a male is brightly coloured but horrible, females are just not interested.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:19.000
And it turned out much later, when I looked at their genetics, what they're really interested in is genes.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:21.000
And it's got nothing to do with the colour.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.000
So it's the males that are colourful?

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.000
Males and females.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.000
So males are incredibly colourful.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.000
So we focus on that.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:35.000
But if you didn't look at a male mandrill and you just saw a female mandrill, you'd notice that she was very pretty and very pink-coloured.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:39.000
So pink nose, blue facial stripes, very pretty.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:43.000
And is it the females that are primarily making the choices?

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.000
It's both.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:47.000
So yes, females definitely choose.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:53.000
Females have the advantage of being much smaller than males, which means that they can get around a lot more easily.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:59.000
I've seen males mate-guarding a tree, thinking that their female is up the tree.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:03.000
When the female has jumped out of the tree about ten minutes ago.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:09.000
And has run down, jumped into another tree, run down, run out of sight and turns up later with another male.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:15.000
So the through line we see is that the male remains the idiot.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:21.000
I'm a female primatologist, so yes, that's the conclusion I draw.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:25.000
And remembers to lift the tail up so it's not dangling down to give away where it is.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:31.000
So mandrills don't have that problem because their tail is only a few inches long.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:39.000
And you mentioned genetics, so in terms of the traits that will be attractive beyond the colour, what are the traits that the females are looking for?

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:43.000
Immune genes, so the strength of the immune system, but not actually that.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.000
How do they gauge that?

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Through smell.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:51.000
Through the immunity genes and the smell of the animal.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:59.000
And they're partly looking just for a good immune system, but they're really looking for a match between their immune system and the male's immune system.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Actually this works both ways.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:07.000
Males also base their mating decisions on the female's genetic makeup.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:11.000
And in the end the point is to make a better quality immune system in the baby.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:15.000
Has that changed in terms of the understanding of humans?

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:25.000
Remember when we had Matthew Cobb on a while ago and he was saying that actually the olfactory senses in humans during dating and mating is not quite what we thought it was before.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:37.000
I think it's changed our understanding of humans in that we're not consciously aware of odour, but we're very unconsciously aware of odour.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:45.000
It affects us enormously, it brings back memories to us instantly, but we don't necessarily think that we're influenced by it.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:51.000
So perhaps what we know now about primates and other mammals has influenced what we know about humans.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:55.000
But we're the ones who shower and put scent on and do stuff.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:20:06.000
So we're messing with a system to try and game it when actually if we left alone we'd probably mate more successfully with people who were better fit for our immune systems.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:24.000
Not entirely because your choice of, well I don't say your choice of perfume, but in general women's choice of perfume, but I guess that applies to aftershaves in the case of men, is directly correlated with your natural smell from your immune system.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:29.000
So you like the perfumes which actually enhance your natural odour.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:44.000
This is why, if I may give you some belated advice Dave, don't buy perfume for your girlfriend, because you'll buy something you like, not the one she likes.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:51.000
Okay, well thank you for giving me a retrospective excuse. I'm never buying perfume for my wife.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:56.000
I've got to be honest with you Dave, I remember when you gave her a box set of Lynx Africa.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:59.000
You were overly happy.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Are you improving your chances? When you choose your perfume and you're saying this will be kind of matching with the smell, is that improving the chance?

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:25.000
In a way possibly, but there are other ways in which we also can pick up on our genes. So one is in the odour that we have on our body, another is in the mouth when we kiss for example.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:33.000
So you might wear some fabulous perfume that is created to attract the opposite sex, but it won't take you as far as kissing.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:38.000
Well obviously I didn't kiss my wife until she was my wife and it was a bit late then.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:45.000
I remember that moment, you opening your mouth going, better have fresh breath, Lynx Africa.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:22:00.000
This leads us on neatly to the next question. As we've just discussed, sexual behaviour in humans is extremely complex. Do we see such complexity in the sexual behaviour?

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:07.000
We see lots of different sexual behaviours, so definitely yes, both across species and within species.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:18.000
And also simply diversity of partner choice, so we have sexual behaviour between males and females, between males and males, between females and females.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:28.000
In some species, like Bonobo is famous for involving all the different age classes too, which we don't do as humans, at least legally we don't do as humans.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:37.000
I know we're mainly talking about monkeys, but if we move on to apes as well, I remember being told a thing about the fact that Bonobos would also have a fashion sense.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:45.000
That they would sometimes pick up a dead rat and wear it on their head and kind of parade around.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:47.000
And that's your idea of fashion?

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:56.000
So things like that, a kind of flamboyant display which includes accessorising.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:02.000
I suspect that monkeys don't need that sort of accessory in the way that Bonobos might, I don't know.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:09.000
Because they have their own accessories, like they have bright red noses and blue stripes on their cheeks and colourful genitalia.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:12.000
Maybe they don't need a rat on their head.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:24.000
It's fascinating, it seems like there you're describing the difference between the mating technique of Adam Ant and Rod Stewart.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:26.000
I've visualised it, absolutely.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.000
Thank you for that.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:35.000
The behaviours we've been talking about, so complex behaviours, but many of them driven by environmental factors and so on.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:42.000
How much can we read into the intelligence of the individual from the social structures and sexual behaviours that we see?

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.000
Depends what you mean by intelligence.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:55.000
That sort of everyday physical world type cognition, everybody has to do in order to live in the real world.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:24:00.000
You've got to be able to compute how far is it from here to where Dave is sitting over there.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:06.000
When I leap off this branch to that branch, do I land in the right place?

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:10.000
I love it when a bit of jeopardy is thrown into the show.

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:14.000
But social intelligence, that's sort of separate.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:20.000
It exploits a lot of the same machinery, if you like, so causal reasoning and stuff.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:27.000
But the key to that seems to be that it involves what's become known as mentalising.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.000
It's the capacity to understand what's going on in somebody else's mind essentially.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.000
Or at least to be able to predict how they're going to behave in future.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:38.000
And to manage and manipulate that to some extent.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:44.000
And that is extremely expensive in terms of neural processing time.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:53.000
And that's why you end up having to have this huge bit of the brain essentially dedicated to managing social relationships.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:58.000
So if you have a bright red nose, you don't need any of that because you can just look at someone else's nose and say,

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:03.000
OK, I know what's going on. A bit of a sniff to figure out all the information.

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:09.000
That's the difference between herding species like feral goats who don't have stable relationships.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:12.000
And don't have stable groups.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:18.000
Because there's no point in learning the ins and outs and foibles of a particular individual.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.000
Because you may never see them again.

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:24.000
All you need to know is, are they a bigger thug than you? Are they prettier than you?

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:27.000
They've got a nice red nose or whatever the cues are.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:31.000
If you're in a stable group you've got to do much more machinating really.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.000
In order to keep everybody in the same place.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:46.000
Because the problem is, as I'm sure you all know, if you're too grumpy with your friends when you go out, they'll abandon you.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:52.000
So if you start being too aggressive within the group, you destroy the group.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:54.000
It's the skills of diplomacy.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:06.000
Monogamy. When we see monkeys that are able to commit to monogamy, will we presume that this shows various other forms of intelligence?

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:11.000
And indeed even possibly in terms of the rearing of the babies?

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:14.000
So there's a lot of coordination involved. Yeah, definitely.

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:18.000
So if you're living just as two and you either spend all your time together,

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:24.000
which can involve coordination and giving up what you wanted to do to coordinate with your pair partner.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:30.000
Or there are some species, lemurs actually rather than monkeys, where they coordinate within a home range but they're not together.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.000
They just keep in touch by yelling at each other.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:37.000
Vocalizing.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:50.000
And the ones that have stable groups, stable monogamous groups as a pair, invariably have bigger brains than the ones that have a pair mating arrangement but don't live together.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:57.000
So long term monogamous relationships and you said there's an increased brain size associated with that.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Does that imply that concepts, very human concepts, like love for example, which you would associate with monogamy.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:11.000
Are we allowed, is it appropriate to begin to think in those terms which are very anthropomorphic?

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:16.000
I mean the answer is yes. If you look at it, it looks very much like what humans do.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:28.000
But I think there is an argument for saying actually the best way to study any system, be it cosmology or physics or humans or animals,

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:35.000
is actually to immerse yourself in it so much that you actually understand it intimately from the inside yourself.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:39.000
Then you have a much better sense of how the thing works.

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:50.000
In some ways the argument that we should be back off from anthropomorphism wasn't a great idea because it divorced you from what the animals are actually doing.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:54.000
The trouble is it's just so hot inside that bonobo costume.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:00.000
Let alone how cramped it is if you want to be in a titty monkey costume.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.000
I would completely agree that we need to immerse ourselves into the lives of primates.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:10.000
It's not typical primatology. There are some people who do it and it's very useful.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:16.000
Then you have the opportunity to describe things like when partner lost for example.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:27.000
If a member of one of those long-term pairs dies, you see in the other what is very obvious to us that we assume is grief.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:33.000
From a more natural science perspective, it's difficult to know how you measure that.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.000
But one thing we do measure is oxytocin.

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:40.000
In those long-term pair bonds, oxytocin is called a bonding hormone sometimes.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:45.000
We see that oxytocin is important in maintaining those bonds.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:50.000
It's also important in bonds even in species that don't have one-to-one bonds.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:58.000
If you call oxytocin a love hormone, which some people do, then we can try to get hold of the idea of love.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:06.000
But also I think there's always a really interesting contrast between what people present in academic conferences and what they talk about in the coffee break.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:13.000
In the coffee break, yes, they'll be talking about how their monkeys love each other because it's so obvious that they do.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:21.000
Last time I visited a facility, one of my friends works with captive American monkeys, these little calatricids.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:27.000
She showed me around and I met all these pairs, male and female, male and female, male and female.

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:31.000
Then finally we got to this cage and she said, oh yeah, this is the two boys.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:34.000
They clearly chose each other and they're hanging out.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:38.000
They have these brilliant enclosures where they all live in jeans.

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:40.000
They love to live in jeans.

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:43.000
There are jeans hanging up in the enclosures.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:47.000
Out of the two legs of these jeans popped out these two little male monkeys.

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:50.000
They've lived together. They just chose to live together.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:56.000
They haven't got babies but she reckons that they would adopt if they were given the opportunity.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:02.000
If Levi 501s don't relaunch with that...

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:06.000
I think at the end of the day, anthropomorphism is a two-edged sword.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:10.000
It's the risk because you can go overboard completely on it.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:17.000
If you're going to study these things, you have to be able to live in this dual world, as it were,

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:25.000
where you can exploit this intuitive understanding that we have of how organisms relate to each other

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:30.000
but at the same time step back and look at it more hard-nosed and sharp.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:33.000
I suppose the hard-nosed and sharp in the end has to come down to...

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:37.000
I don't think we'll ever get at it studying it with behaviour, maybe, I don't know,

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:44.000
but my guess is we'll only really know if we can pick it up in the same bits of the brain firing up

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:49.000
and the same surges of hormones in the system in the brain,

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:52.000
so things like oxytocin and endorphins and stuff,

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:58.000
and the way those flood the brain during the course of interactions.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:59.000
And they are the same.

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:04.000
I mean, the endorphin system and the oxytocin system are the same in monkeys and humans.

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:08.000
So you're saying a new technique, a new frontier of knowledge,

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:14.000
would be really to look at the brain of a monkey, let's say,

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:20.000
so we want to see how the neurons fire, if the patterns are the same,

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:23.000
the regions of the brain that are stimulated are the same as a human,

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:26.000
that would be the final proof,

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:31.000
strong evidence that they're thinking in the same way and experiencing the same feelings.

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:33.000
I think it's closing the gap,

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:38.000
but I don't think you'll ever get to that final point where you know what's going on.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:42.000
I use the word feelings there, so that's the point, isn't it, the root of it.

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:47.000
Because, I mean, bear in mind, at the end of the day, we have this problem with ourselves.

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:52.000
In other words, we tend to see the world as being populated by people like me.

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:55.000
And I understand, because that's the only reference point I have,

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:59.000
is how I think and feel inside me,

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:04.000
and we kind of generalise that onto other people and assume they're operating in the same way.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:11.000
But, Joe, you said you don't think we'd ever get to that point where we'd say that the map is now so obvious.

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:15.000
Let's say you had a family group and you see exactly how the brains are operating,

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.000
then the patterns in the brain are happening.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:23.000
I just think we might be happy with an explanation until we develop some new method

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:27.000
and you could go further into trying to explain what's happening in the brain

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:31.000
or what's happening in the endocrine system or anything else that you might be interested in.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:35.000
And you suddenly realise that you had an approximation of an answer,

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:38.000
but there's still more to do and further to go.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:42.000
Even when I say we might be closing the gap, I think the gap is just there.

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:48.000
It is the fact that we have to eventually say the patterns are the same.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:52.000
So we are assuming that the experience is the same.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:56.000
But we'll never know if the experience is the same.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:59.000
I think we've done a very English thing with this show.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:04.000
We've somehow really dragged out the bit that has allowed us to avoid talking about sex.

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:07.000
It's a desperate bid that we won't get to it.

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:10.000
But we promised this show would include monkey sex.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:14.000
Please welcome Anita and Beau.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:18.000
So to get now to that, to the actual sexual behaviour.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:20.000
The nitty gritties.

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:25.000
So the first thing is, do we get a sense, Jo, that monkeys enjoy sex?

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:27.000
That this is fun?

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:28.000
Yeah, definitely.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:30.000
So if we talk about females, I'm female.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.000
Many female primates clearly show signs of orgasm.

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:38.000
So again, if we assume that orgasm is fun because for individuals it seems to be fun,

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:40.000
then, yeah, they're having fun.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:42.000
They also seek out sex.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.000
Both males and females seek out sex.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:45.000
Not all the time.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:47.000
And it varies between the individuals.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:50.000
But they seek it out, which suggests that it's fun.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:57.000
So there's not, say, a pattern in terms of, say, the menstrual cycle of the monkey?

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:59.000
Or that they would be?

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:03.000
It's not merely, oh, hang on a minute, I can have a baby now.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:06.000
That you actually see it as in the same way with humans that people go,

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:09.000
do you know what, it's enough fun to not worry too much about the outcome.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:12.000
Loads of reasons for having sex.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:15.000
So yes, of course, sex in order to reproduce.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.000
But loads of other reasons too.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:22.000
You started with a story about a monkey in a tire at the zoo by itself,

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:26.000
which suggests that it is fun.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.000
Is that the tire element of it?

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.000
The solo element of it suggests that that must be fun.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:34.000
It can't be doing it because it thinks it's going to lead to a baby.

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:36.000
So it must be enjoying itself.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:41.000
Yes, so evolutionarily, perhaps there obviously is a link to reproduction.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:43.000
But there's a link to many other things too.

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:46.000
In some cases, social bonds between males and females,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:49.000
between males and males, between females and females, and so on.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:52.000
But yes, definitely, as far as we know, it's fun.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:57.000
And in terms of things like, you know, sometimes you'll see these evangelical preachers,

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:03.000
televangelists or whatever, going on about the fact that we don't see homosexuality in any other animal.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:10.000
Which means they've never even owned a dog, to be honest.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:16.000
So again, in terms of same-sex relations, sexual relations in the monkey world.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:21.000
So first of all, there are plenty of same-sex relationships in the monkey world.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:26.000
I suppose also we should say that when we come to talking about same-sex relationships in humans,

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:29.000
whether or not animals do that is completely irrelevant.

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:33.000
But if we want to look and determine whether animals do it, primates do it, yes, lots.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:36.000
It varies across the species, but yeah, a lot.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:38.000
But I think that's in some ways why it's good to know.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:43.000
As you go, this is not, you know, people will describe certain things as being against nature.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:46.000
When in fact you go, let us look at the natural world and we see...

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:49.000
It's everywhere in the natural world, absolutely.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:54.000
Dave, I suppose the big question for you really is, and we need your definition, what is love?

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:57.000
LAUGHTER

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:01.000
It's committing to not kissing your wife until you're married.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:06.000
Who knows what love is?

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:14.000
Even in our own lives most of us believe we're in love and later can reassess that and go,

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:18.000
that wasn't it actually, because until you've experienced it...

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:23.000
So teenagers fall in love, but it's not, it's a kind of infatuation.

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:29.000
Later in life when you feel more deeply in love you are able to look back on that and go,

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:33.000
I just really liked her. It's not the same, is it?

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:36.000
What I was feeling was an excitement for novelty.

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:41.000
But it's how do you possibly define what it is until it's too late?

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:44.000
LAUGHTER

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:48.000
What you've described there eloquently is how complex these ideas are.

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:55.000
We are getting quite close to the end, but Jo, in terms of what is love when you're looking at monkeys,

00:36:55.000 --> 00:37:02.000
how would you go, this appears to be what I will define for this piece of research as a loving relationship?

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:08.000
Oh, well, I would never get away with calling it a loving relationship in a scientific article, I don't think.

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:13.000
Maybe people will in future, but when you write the popular book, that's different.

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:16.000
Right. And is that available yet?

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:21.000
I've got a feeling that humans have somehow gamed the system,

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:26.000
because in almost all other creatures it's the males who are the flamboyantly coloured,

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:29.000
you know, sort of the peacock tail or whatever.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:33.000
And in humans it is the men who grow the beards and do whatever,

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:37.000
but most of us shave them off, have a haircut and say, put some make-up on, love.

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:41.000
And we've somehow gamed the system and gone, make yourself pretty.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:44.000
You put the colour on, we're not doing it any more.

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:49.000
It's a very culture-bound, historically time-bound phenomenon.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:55.000
There are instances of cultures around the world where men wear make-up and perform for the women to choose.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:38:01.000
Yeah, which feels like the way it should be, but thank God we've got away from that.

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:03.000
We've gone too lazy for that.

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:06.000
We have just about run out of time. Just to give you some insight into the scripts,

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.000
I'm looking through, is there any question we haven't asked?

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:09.000
All of them.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:11.000
There's one question we haven't asked, it's right at the end.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:17.000
In this very sophisticated discussion we've had, if you could be any species of monkey, what would it be?

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:23.000
That is the question that's written here, so I feel duty-bound to ask it.

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:26.000
Which species of monkey would you be, eminent professor?

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:29.000
Let's go Robin, Joe, David.

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:30.000
Robin?

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:36.000
That's really difficult, for males anyway, because they usually end up in fights which they lose very badly.

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:38.000
You can be a goat if you want.

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:41.000
Well, Lara's bad.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:46.000
I spend a lot of time working on a very small miniature antelope called a Clipspringer.

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:54.000
It's intensely pair-bonded. It's the most loyally pair-bonded species anywhere in the world.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:57.000
It's such a relaxed, cosy life that they have.

00:38:57.000 --> 00:39:04.000
If you see one of them, the other member of the pair will be within 3 or 4 metres, always.

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:08.000
That's an antelope, right?

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:11.000
I'm giving up on monkeys.

00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:13.000
Monkeys are too violent.

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:14.000
Joe, which monkey?

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:17.000
I would be a dominant female mandrill for two reasons.

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:20.000
One is I think they have the best life.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:25.000
They're totally in charge. They know they're going to be in charge for the whole of their lives.

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:29.000
No-one gives them any trouble. That's the first reason.

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:34.000
The second reason is then I'd know what it was like to be a female mandrill.

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:39.000
David?

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:43.000
I think the Clipspringer.

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:47.000
That does sound appealing, but I am scared of heights.

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:51.000
So I'll go for being a female mandrill as well.

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:54.000
Because who am I to argue with an expert?

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:58.000
I'm the dominant one. You can be the next one down in the hierarchy.

00:39:59.000 --> 00:40:02.000
But we'll be in separate groups.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.000
It'll be OK.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:06.000
So we also asked our audience a question and we said,

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:09.000
what is the most curious thing you've seen at the zoo?

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:14.000
This one's very nice, isn't it? An inquisitive child.

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:20.000
I have one says, a small monkey stole a tea bag out of a lady's handbag at London Zoo.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:25.000
Which just goes to show those PG Tips adverts were very, very persuasive.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:28.000
I did those PG Tips adverts. You know the old, if you hum it, I'll play it?

00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:32.000
And it was chimps dressed in human clothes and things.

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:36.000
The way they did that, they put peanut butter on the gums of the monkeys

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:39.000
so that they would move their lips and then they had actors lip sync it.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:43.000
It's a technology they now use in Hollyoaks.

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:51.000
My wife, she was a keeper.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:58.000
This is like a complaint, a letter of complaint.

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:01.000
One of the ones we get from Radio 4 listeners.

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:04.000
It goes on for a long time and it's in green ink.

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:07.000
I watch an obnoxious couple get too close to an enclosure,

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:11.000
ignoring numerous signs of warning about the hippo's curious habit

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:14.000
of smashing their tails to spread their excrement widely.

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:18.000
I enjoyed watching them sprayed by flinging poo.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:22.000
For them, flings could only get better.

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:26.000
I've just got a Brian Coxalotl.

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:29.000
I've heard that rumour.

00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:35.000
Thanks to our panel, Joe Setchell, Robin Dunbar and Dave Gorman.

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:46.000
Next week, it's Christmas and thanks to Brian's air miles,

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:49.000
we're going to the North Pole to meet Father Christmas and his reindeer.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:52.000
So we're going to ask how exactly do reindeer evolve to fly

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:56.000
and what are the health ramifications of eating two billion mince pies

00:41:56.000 --> 00:41:59.000
and drinking a billion glasses of sherry in one night?

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:03.000
Or how do living things survive in polar regions?

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:05.000
It'll be one or the other. It'll be one or the other.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:07.000
Flying reindeer, living things in polar regions.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:09.000
Thank you. Bye-bye. Bye.

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:11.000
APPLAUSE

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:18.000
Do you want that nice again?

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:20.000
Hi, it's India here.

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:23.000
I'm very excited to bring you The Return of Child.

00:42:23.000 --> 00:42:26.000
So we've been on the journey of an embryo

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:29.000
all the way to a baby's first birthday

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:34.000
and now we are going to enter the explosive life of the toddler

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:37.000
because this is the perfect place to unpick

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:40.000
the very complicated world of emotions,

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:42.000
the emotions that affect us all.

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:45.000
So come with us as over eight episodes

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:48.000
we fall through the abundant and dizzying world of happiness,

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.000
descend into the depths of fear

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:53.000
and the gendered and dangerous world of anger

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:56.000
and then crawl, wobble and bounce our way

00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:00.000
through awe, love, anxiety and surprise.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.000
From BBC Radio 4, this is Child with me, India Rackerson.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:06.000
Listen first on BBC Sounds.

